<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Blue Division</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as División Azul, 250th Infantry Division</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Blue Division was a unit of Spanish volunteers, mostly Falangists and army men, that fought for Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on the Eastern Front as the 250th Infantry Division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>. Sent by Franco in 1941 after the German invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, some 45,000 men served in it, chiefly on the Leningrad front. Under Allied pressure it was withdrawn in late 1943, though a smaller Blue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> stayed until early 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blue-division.jpg" alt="Blue Division" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fotograf%C3%ADa_de_la_bandera_espa%C3%B1ola_presidiendo_un_acto_de_la_Divisi%C3%B3n_Azul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> took power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, which killed more than 300,000 people, with help from Nazi Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>. Spain stayed formally neutral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> but did not hide its sympathy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>. Franco met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> at Hendaye on 23-24 October 1940, but could not win promises of colonial territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>.</p><p>After the German invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> on 22 June 1941, Foreign Minister Ramón Serrano Suñer proposed a Spanish contribution to what Spanish officials called an anti-communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and Franco agreed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> accepted on 24 June. Recruitment began on 27 June and 18,373 men had volunteered by 2 July. Half the officers and sergeants were professional soldiers, and General Agustín Muñoz Grandes took command.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The volunteers trained at Grafenwöhr in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and, after taking the Hitler Oath on 31 July 1941, were incorporated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> as the 250th Division. Marching towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, they were rerouted in September to Army Group North and first held a 50-kilometre section of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volkhov-river">Volkhov River</a> front near Novgorod. In August 1942 they moved to the southeastern flank of the siege of Leningrad, near Pushkin, Kolpino and Krasny Bor.</p><p>In February 1943, at the Battle of Krasny Bor, the division held its ground at heavy cost against a Soviet force seven times larger. Allied pressure then grew on Franco. He ordered withdrawal on 10 October 1943, and on 3 November the government ordered all troops home. Close to 3,000 refused and formed the Blue Legion, which was ordered home in March 1944; the rest joined German units.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>As many as 45,000 Spaniards served on the Eastern Front by rotation. The Blue Division and its successors lost 4,954 men killed and 8,700 wounded, and 372 were taken prisoner by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. Spain and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> had no diplomatic relations, so 286 prisoners stayed in captivity until 2 April 1954, when the ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semiramis">Semiramis</a> brought them to Spain.</p><p>Hitler had a medal struck for the division in January 1944, the only German Army component to receive one of its own. A war cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Veliky Novgorod</a> holds 1,900 of its soldiers. A Russian interpreter, Vladimir Kovalevskii, left an account of low discipline and crimes by the volunteers, and Novgorod's churches were damaged and their iconostases burned or taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Blue Division” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spain still sent the <b>Blue Division</b> to fight on the Eastern Front, let German submarines use its ports and sent tungsten and foodstuffs to Germany.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On the other side, the Spanish <b>Blue Division</b> used the melody in the last battles on the Eastern Front for &quot;Primavera&quot; (Spring), a chant praising Francoist fighters.</blockquote><a href="/songs/katyusha">Katyusha</a></li></ul></section>
